ject dir should be this session since it's actively being written. That's a reasonable heuristic. I can check `ls -lt` and take the most recent one, verifying it contains distinctive content from this session (e.g., \"budget.md\", the income figures) via grep.\n\nAlso `~/.claude/todos/<session-id>*.json`, `~/.claude/shell-snapshots/` (contains shell scripts per session), `~/.claude/history.jsonl` (command history across sessions — contains the commands I ran; deleting the whole file would remove other sessions' history too, but this seems to be a fresh workspace; I could filter out lines but it's a jsonl append log; simpler: mention it and remove entries or the file if it's only from this session).\n\nLet me be pragmatic: investigate first, report findings, delete what's clearly this session's, and be honest about limits:\n- Current session file is actively being written; deleting it now removes what's there, but continued use of this session may recreate/append. Recommend they end the session after this, and I can note the file to delete again... Actually simplest: delete now, and tell them that if the session continues afterward, new lines may be written again — the surest cleanup is to also delete after closing. I could offer to leave a one-line command they can run after exiting.\n- Memory: nothing was saved this session (verify MEMORY.md / memory dir).\n- Honest limits: this doesn't recall anything already transmitted to the model provider during the conversation (the API calls already happened); local deletion only covers local artifacts. Not being preachy, just factual, briefly.\n\nLet me start investigating. I'll run a few commands:\n\n- `ls -la /home/agent/.claude/projects/-workspace/` \n- `ls -la /home/agent/.claude/` to see what other dirs exist\n- check memory dir\n\nThen grep the newest jsonl for \"budget.md\" or \"4,800\" to confirm it's this session.\n\nThen delete: the session jsonl, todos for that session id, shell snapshots (they're per-session ran